<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Choose wide, long or person-period data; handle irregular measurements and missing
periods; reshape privately; and fit a discrete-time hazard model and repeated-measure
mixed models across two connections.
</div>

**You need:** [Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start),
[output formats](book-08-outputs.html) and [lab values](book-15-labs.html).
Start a fresh session in `book-work` and run every section in order, including solutions.
GiBleed and Synthea are public **synthetic** resources. Their results teach analysis
mechanics, not clinical conclusions. This chapter uses dsBaseClient 6.3.5.

## The same people, two shapes

*Longitudinal* means following the same people over time. Here is a tiny invented
picture, **not a DataSHIELD row release**. The same three fictional people have
these measurements; days are relative to each person's entry.

| Person | Value on day 0 | Value on day 30 |
|---|---:|---:|
| A | 14 | 15 |
| B | 13 | NA |
| C | 15 | 14 |

Turning the two measurement columns into rows gives this long picture:

| Person | Day | Value |
|---|---:|---:|
| A | 0 | 14 |
| A | 30 | 15 |
| B | 0 | 13 |
| B | 30 | NA |
| C | 0 | 15 |
| C | 30 | 14 |

This is a scheduled grid: B's second row explicitly represents a missing value.
An event-long table would omit B's day-30 row if no event occurred. Its absence
must not be interpreted as a measured zero. Six rows still represent three people.

**Exercise.** Which shape would you use to compare baseline means? Which for a
model of change over time?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

Use the wide day-0 column for baseline means. Use long data with a person key and
time for repeated-measure models. Account for dependence between the same person's
rows. Taking an ordinary mean over long rows weights frequent attenders more.

</details>

## Choose the shape before requesting it

| Question | Shape | What a row means |
|---|---|---|
| Baseline lab or cross-sectional comparison | wide | One person or entry episode |
| How do repeated readings vary with time? | long | One observed measurement, with time since index |
| Did an outcome occur during this at-risk period? | person_period | One person-episode and time bin |
| How long until the first outcome or censoring? | survival | One follow-up record |
| When was an exposure active? | intervals | One exposure start and end |

Time-varying covariates belong to periods: use information available by that
period's start. A future lab mean would leak future information into a hazard model.
A survival output must specify censoring. Intervals retain both ends of an exposure;
they are not automatically nonoverlapping. Chapter 8 executes all five shapes.

Start with GiBleed and request visit rows directly from a recipe.

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
source("tools/book/practice.R")
practice <- book_connect("gibleed")
conns <- practice$conns
visits <- omop_event("visit_occurrence", concepts = 9201L, name = "admission")
visitors <- omop_population("visitors", index = omop_index(visits))
visit_recipe <- omop_recipe(events = list(visits), populations = list(visitors),
  outputs = omop_output("visits", rows = "visitors", format = "long", sources = "admission",
    options = list(columns_derived = c("days_from_index", "duration_days"))))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(visit_recipe, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(visit_recipe, out = c(visits = "D_visit_long"), conns = conns))
```

The long table remains private. Release only the named visit-type counts and
column names, which describe the representation.

```{r visit-shape}
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_visit_long", datasources = conns)
visit_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_visit_long$concept_id", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(visit_counts))
```

**Exercise.** Summarise the time since entry. Does a long format guarantee repeated visits?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r visit-exercise}
visit_time <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean("D_visit_long$days_from_index", type = "combine", datasources = conns)
visit_time
```

No. Here the mean is about 1273 days: the first admission contributes zero,
and subsequent admissions contribute positive offsets. Some people still have
only one admission. This is an event-weighted mean, not the mean gap between
admissions. Switch to Synthea for repeated numeric measurements.

</details>

## Irregular measurements: define windows first

```{r numeric-connection}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
DSI::datashield.logout(conns)
practice <- book_connect("numeric")
conns <- practice$conns
lab <- omop_event("measurement", concepts = 3000963L, value = "value_as_number",
  where = omop_where(unit_concept_id == 8713L), name = "hb")
entry <- omop_population("entry", index = omop_index(lab))
class(entry)
```

Index is each person's first hemoglobin. These synthetic records are irregularly
spaced over years. Use three **1000-day windows**, not calendar years. Window 1
includes days 0–999, window 2 days 1000–1999, and window 3 days 2000–2999.
First and last refer to actual dates within each window, not scheduled visits.

```{r window-variables}
window_value <- function(which, start, end) {
  omop_variable(event = lab, reducer = omop_reduce(which,
    window = omop_window(start, end)))
}
windows <- omop_recipe(events = list(lab), populations = list(entry), variables = list(
  first1 = window_value("first_value", 0, 999),
  first2 = window_value("first_value", 1000, 1999),
  first3 = window_value("first_value", 2000, 2999),
  last1 = window_value("last_value", 0, 999),
  last2 = window_value("last_value", 1000, 1999),
  last3 = window_value("last_value", 2000, 2999)),
  outputs = omop_output("wide", rows = "entry", format = "wide"))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(windows, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(windows, out = c(wide = "D_windows"), conns = conns))
```

No eligible nonmissing value gives `NA`. It never gives zero hemoglobin. Before
analysing a period, count its missing values and summarise its first and last
readings. The following loop repeats the same three explicit aggregate calls.

```{r window-summaries}
window_results <- lapply(1:3, function(period) {
  first_name <- paste0("D_windows$first", period)
  last_name <- paste0("D_windows$last", period)
  first_mean <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean(first_name, type = "combine", datasources = conns)
  last_mean <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean(last_name, type = "combine", datasources = conns)
  data.frame(Period = period,
    First = first_mean$Global.Mean[1, 1], Last = last_mean$Global.Mean[1, 1],
    Observed = first_mean$Global.Mean[1, "Nvalid"], Missing = first_mean$Global.Mean[1, "Nmissing"])
})
knitr::kable(do.call(rbind, window_results))
```

These means use the observed people in each period; changing means can therefore
reflect changing membership. A missing measurement does not prove that the person
left observation. Check observation coverage separately for a follow-up analysis.

**Exercise.** Make missingness a named category for each period and release its counts.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r missingness-exercise}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_windows",
  missing1 = ifelse(is.na(first1), "missing", "observed"),
  missing2 = ifelse(is.na(first2), "missing", "observed"),
  missing3 = ifelse(is.na(first3), "missing", "observed"),
  newobj = "D_missing", conns = conns))
missing_counts <- lapply(1:3, function(period) {
  result <- dsBaseClient::ds.table(paste0("D_missing$missing", period), datasources = conns)
  transform(book_counts(result), Period = period)
})
knitr::kable(do.call(rbind, missing_counts))
```

The source checks find 28, 10 and 15 observed people: therefore 0, 18 and 13
missing values. Do not carry a measurement forward without declaring how long
it remains valid and what that assumption means.

</details>

## Ask the recipe, or reshape on the server

The installed `ds.reShape()` can turn existing wide columns into period rows.
The native recipe uses names without dots. Create three private aliases with the
`first.1`, `first.2`, `first.3` names expected by this dsBase reshape method.
This operation assigns privately; it never returns individual rows.

```{r reshape}
stopifnot(all(c("ds.reShape", "ds.lmerSLMA", "ds.glmerSLMA") %in%
                getNamespaceExports("dsBaseClient")))
for (period in 1:3) {
  dsBaseClient::ds.asNumeric(x.name = paste0("D_windows$first", period),
    newobj = paste0("first.", period), datasources = conns)
}
dsBaseClient::ds.dataFrame(c("D_windows$person_id", "first.1", "first.2", "first.3"),
  newobj = "D_for_reshape", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.reShape("D_for_reshape", varying = c("first.1", "first.2", "first.3"),
  timevar.name = "period", idvar.name = "person_id", direction = "long",
  newobj = "D_reshaped", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_reshaped", datasources = conns)
```

This gives each person three period rows, including missing first values.
Only the person key and three first values enter this reshape; last values are
not copied into every period. Its `period` is 1, 2 or 3, **not an
actual measurement date**. A reshape cannot recover dates or readings discarded
by a reducer. `ds.reShape` also depends on the server's allowed privacy profile;
this run uses the installed practice configuration.

For a dense panel with last values and event counts, ask the recipe directly.
The native panel supports `last` and `mean`, but not a `first` analysis. The wide
window reducers above provide first values explicitly without inventing a panel option.

```{r recipe-panel}
panel <- omop_recipe(events = list(lab), populations = list(entry),
  outputs = omop_output("panel", rows = "entry", format = "person_period",
    options = list(sources = list(lab), bins = list(width = 1000L),
      window = omop_window(0, 2999), analyses = c("count", "last"), representation = "dense")))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(panel, conns = conns)$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(panel, out = c(panel = "D_panel"), conns = conns))
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_panel", datasources = conns)
```

The panel respects observation coverage and retains empty observed bins: a zero
count and a missing last value describe different things. It can omit time outside
coverage, so it need not have the same roster as the wide-window reshape. Requesting
the right shape keeps keys, windows, coverage and event semantics in one checked plan.

**Exercise.** Verify that the reshape kept three rows per person, without reading rows.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r reshape-exercise}
period_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_reshaped$period", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(period_counts))
```

There are 28 rows in each period, including rows with missing measurements.
That is 84 rows, still 28 people. A row-count increase is not a sample-size increase.

</details>

## Build a risk set for a discrete-time hazard

A *hazard* is the probability of an outcome in the current period among people
still at risk at its start. Here the outcome is the **next recorded hemoglobin**,
not illness or death. Start on day 1 after the first reading, and stop at the first
subsequent reading, death, observation end or day 7300, whichever comes first.
The absence of a repeat lab is not a normal result.

For this section use the same disjoint public Synthea split as chapter 15:
14 people at each of two local DSLite servers. No people are duplicated. A real
multisite study must establish nonoverlapping populations separately.

```{r two-sites}
pair <- book_numeric_pair()
two_conns <- pair$conns
names(two_conns)
```

The panel already stops at observation coverage. Attach one first-outcome day
and one death day per episode, using a checked many-to-one join. These dates
are index-relative numbers and stay private.

```{r hazard-recipe}
death <- omop_event("death", name = "death")
hazard <- omop_recipe(events = list(lab, death), populations = list(entry), variables = list(
  next_day = omop_variable(event = lab,
    reducer = omop_reduce("first_date", window = omop_window(1, NULL))),
  death_day = omop_variable(event = death, reducer = omop_reduce("first_date"))),
  outputs = list(
    omop_output("daily", rows = "entry", format = "person_period",
      options = list(sources = list(lab), bins = list(width = 1L),
        window = omop_window(1, 7300), analyses = "count", representation = "dense")),
    omop_output("ends", rows = "entry", format = "wide"),
    omop_output("risk", rows = "entry", format = "joined",
      options = list(from = list(list(output = "daily"), list(output = "ends")),
        how = "left", cardinality = "many_to_one"))))
hazard$publish <- "risk"
stopifnot(recipe_explain(hazard, conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool")$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(hazard, out = c(risk = "D_days"), conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool"))
```

Remove days after the first repeat or death. A missing next-day value means
“no subsequent lab found”, so retain observed days until another censor applies.
We count an event on the death day if both dates coincide; state this tie rule.

```{r hazard-risk-set}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_days", to_next = next_day - period_start_days,
  to_death = death_day - period_start_days,
  newobj = "D_remaining", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool"))
print(ds.omop.filter("D_remaining",
  expr = omop_where((is.na(to_next) | to_next >= 0) &
  (is.na(to_death) | to_death >= 0)),
  newobj = "D_risk", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool"))
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_risk", event = ifelse(hb_count > 0, 1L, 0L),
  years = period_start_days / 365.25,
  newobj = "D_hazard", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool"))
hazard_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_hazard$event", datasources = two_conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(hazard_counts))
```

Each row is one observed day at risk. More than one lab on that day is still one
outcome. Time in years is a predictor; the outcome probability remains **per day**.
The binomial GLM models log odds with a linear time trend. A study would assess
that time specification and the assumption of noninformative censoring.

The pinned dsBase 6.3.5 local setup fits use one iteration and emit the explained
`glm.fit: algorithm did not converge` warnings. The distributed fit then iterates;
inspect its final diagnostics, which the book checks against a central fit of the
public source. The warnings below are retained, not treated as evidence of success.

<!-- expected-glm-warning:start -->
```{r hazard-model}
hazard_fit <- dsBaseClient::ds.glm("event ~ years", data = "D_hazard",
  family = "binomial", datasources = two_conns)
hazard_table <- as.data.frame(hazard_fit$coefficients[, 1:4, drop = FALSE])
hazard_table[["p-value"]] <- format.pval(hazard_table[["p-value"]], digits = 3, eps = 1e-16)
knitr::kable(hazard_table, digits = 4)
```
<!-- expected-glm-warning:end -->

```{r hazard-diagnostics}
list(iterations = hazard_fit$iter, valid_days = hazard_fit$Nvalid,
     disclosure_risk = hazard_fit$disclosure.risk, errors = hazard_fit$errorMessage)
```

This is a jointly estimated distributed GLM. Its risk-set likelihood has one
first event at most per person; it is not a logistic model of every recurrent
reading treated as an independent patient.

**Exercise.** Show the fitted odds ratio per additional year since entry. Does it
mean a yearly probability of getting a lab?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r hazard-exercise}
exp(hazard_fit$coefficients["years", 1])
```

No. It compares the odds of a repeat **on the current day**, conditional on having
remained at risk, for a one-year difference in time since entry. It is not a causal
effect or an annual risk. The event is recorded care, not disease onset.

</details>

## Repeated measures and mixed models

For actual repeated-value modelling use Synthea's BMI readings, whose within-person
trajectories provide a more useful random-intercept example than hemoglobin here.
A *random intercept* lets people have different baseline levels and accounts for
correlation between their repeated readings. This is a different question from
time to the first repeat lab.

```{r repeated-recipe}
bmi <- omop_event("measurement", concepts = 3038553L, value = "value_as_number",
  where = omop_where(unit_concept_id == 9531L), name = "bmi")
bmi_entry <- omop_population("bmi_entry", index = omop_index(bmi))
repeated <- omop_recipe(events = list(bmi), populations = list(bmi_entry),
  outputs = omop_output("long", rows = "bmi_entry", format = "long", sources = "bmi",
    options = list(columns_derived = c("value", "unit", "days_since_previous"))))
stopifnot(recipe_explain(repeated, conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool")$selected == "v2")
print(recipe_execute(repeated, out = c(long = "D_long"), conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool"))
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_long", datasources = two_conns)
```

`start_date` is days since first BMI under the default relative-date policy.
The person key, all readings and their dates stay on the servers. Preserve actual
time; coding visit numbers as equally spaced would misrepresent these records.
Each person has exactly one indexed episode here, so `cohort_row_id` is the
person-level grouping key for these models. Using this stable episode key also
keeps the local teaching runs reproducible when temporary resource paths change
pseudonym labels. With multiple episodes per person, use a person-level key to
model their dependence instead.

```{r repeated-variables}
print(ds.omop.mutate("D_long", years = start_date / 365.25,
  above_cutoff = ifelse(value >= 25, 1L, 0L),
  newobj = "D_repeat", conns = two_conns, symbol = "book_pool"))
repeat_mean <- dsBaseClient::ds.mean("D_repeat$value", type = "combine", datasources = two_conns)
repeat_counts <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_repeat$above_cutoff", datasources = two_conns)
knitr::kable(book_group_comparison(repeat_counts, repeat_mean, "BMI reading", "kg/m²"))
```

This mean weights readings, not people. The cutoff is a teaching rule only: this
synthetic population includes children, and an adult BMI cutoff is not an age-specific
clinical classification. The continuous and binary responses answer different questions.

`SLMA` means **study-level meta-analysis**. Each server fits its own mixed model;
only estimates and standard errors return for pooling. It is not a single mixed
model fitted jointly to concatenated rows. Here we show fixed-effect meta-analysis
columns (`pooled.FE`, `se.FE`); this assumes a common coefficient across sites.
The method also returns ML/REML random-effects pooling, with little information
about between-site heterogeneity from only two sites.

The installed dsBase calls `lme4::nobars`, which in this dependency version emits
a deprecation warning about moving to `reformulas`. It concerns the upstream API,
not the data. We retain and explain that exact warning; model convergence and
singularity are checked separately rather than suppressing diagnostics.

<!-- expected-mixed-warning:start -->
```{r linear-mixed}
linear_mixed <- dsBaseClient::ds.lmerSLMA("value ~ years + (1|cohort_row_id)",
  dataName = "D_repeat", datasources = two_conns)
knitr::kable(linear_mixed$SLMA.pooled.ests.matrix[, c("pooled.FE", "se.FE"), drop = FALSE])
linear_mixed$Convergence.error.message
```
<!-- expected-mixed-warning:end -->

The time coefficient is BMI units per year under this linear trajectory model.
Real growth may be nonlinear; these data do not validate a constant-slope assumption.
No individual random intercepts are downloaded.

```{r binary-mixed}
binary_mixed <- dsBaseClient::ds.glmerSLMA("above_cutoff ~ years + (1|cohort_row_id)",
  family = "binomial", dataName = "D_repeat", datasources = two_conns)
knitr::kable(binary_mixed$SLMA.pooled.ests.matrix[, c("pooled.FE", "se.FE"), drop = FALSE])
binary_mixed$Convergence.error.message
```

This coefficient is on the log-odds scale for a reading above the teaching cutoff,
conditional on the person's random intercept. It is neither a BMI-unit slope nor
the first-event hazard from the preceding section.

**Exercise 1.** Show the number of contributing sites and the binary-model time
odds ratio. Is SLMA pooling equivalent to the preceding distributed GLM?

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>

```{r mixed-exercise}
list(sites = binary_mixed$num.valid.studies,
     time_odds_ratio = exp(binary_mixed$SLMA.pooled.ests.matrix["years", "pooled.FE"]))
```

No. SLMA combines site coefficients and their uncertainty. The distributed GLM
combines iteration statistics to estimate one model. Both need compatible variable
meaning and disjoint populations; they solve different estimation problems.

</details>

**Exercise 2.** Would a singular fit be proof that a mixed model worked well?

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

No. A near-zero random-effect variance or another boundary estimate can make the
random-effects structure unsupported. Inspect the site's convergence messages,
variance components, observation counts and design. Simplify or reconsider the
model for a reason; do not remove warnings merely to obtain a clean table.

</details>

## Recap

Choose the row meaning first. Keep actual time for irregular readings; distinguish
missing values, absent events and time outside observation. Request the recipe's
shape when possible, and understand what information a reshape cannot recover.
A first-event hazard needs an at-risk panel with follow-up stopped at the event.
Repeated-measure models instead retain repeated readings and model their dependence.
Check every site's diagnostics and distinguish joint GLM estimation from SLMA pooling.
